{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_213cfcafcc24",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 105,
      "mechanism": "The reset synchronization chain requires 3 cycles (input_rst_sync1/2/3) to propagate async_rst de-assertion. During cycles 1-3, input_rst_sync2=1 holds the FIFO in reset, but input_axis_tready=~full exposes the uninitialized full flag (1). Qualifying readiness with !output_rst_sync2 masks tready until the output domain acknowledges reset (output_rst_sync2=0 at cycle 4), aligning the 3-cycle input reset latency with the 2-cycle output reset latency."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_ac60e3091721",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 105,
      "mechanism": "input_rst_sync2 is the middle stage of the 3-cycle input reset synchronizer. At cycle 4, input_rst_sync2=0 while input_rst_sync3=1 (reset still active for state). Adding input_rst_sync2 to the readiness gate masks tready during the cycle where the write pointer would otherwise advance on stale full=0, preventing premature write before input_rst_sync3 clears at cycle 5."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_3d75e9177240",
      "failure_port": "output_axis_tdata",
      "source_line": 110,
      "mechanism": "The output domain reset check uses async_rst directly (line 110), bypassing the 3-cycle output_rst_sync chain. This causes output logic to exit reset at cycle 1 while input domain remains in reset until cycle 5. Replacing async_rst with (async_rst && !input_rst_sync3) couples output reset exit to input domain reset completion, synchronizing the first valid read (cycle 8) with expected data=1."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_e4d895e08cad",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 105,
      "mechanism": "input_rst_sync3 is the final stage of the input reset synchronizer (clears at cycle 5). Qualifying ~full with !input_rst_sync3 extends the tready mask by one cycle vs patch_ac60e3091721, covering the exact cycle where wr_ptr would increment on the first valid write. This directly gates the cycle-4 tready spike but may over-mask if input_rst_sync3 timing shifts."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_9425e1a524ed",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 139,
      "mechanism": "The write enable (line 139) lacks reset gating, allowing mem[wr_ptr] update at cycle 4 when input_rst_sync3=1. Adding && input_rst_sync3 to the write condition blocks the premature write that corrupts the first output data (cycle 8 actual=2 vs expected=1). This targets the data path directly rather than the ready signal."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_061a354d3ba4",
      "failure_port": "output_axis_tdata",
      "source_line": 122,
      "mechanism": "The input domain reset check (line 122) uses async_rst directly, causing wr_ptr/wr_ptr_gray to clear at cycle 1 instead of cycle 5 (input_rst_sync3). Replacing async_rst with (async_rst && write) is ineffective (write=0 during reset), but the family concept—gating input reset with a domain-local condition—points to the correct fix: use input_rst_sync3 instead of async_rst at line 122."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_8b896b0b2e36",
      "failure_port": "output_axis_tdata",
      "source_line": 160,
      "mechanism": "The output domain valid_reg update (line 160) checks output_rst_sync3 but the write pointer sync (wr_ptr_gray_sync2) depends on input_rst_sync3. Adding && input_rst_sync3 to the output_rst_sync3 check ensures valid_reg only asserts after both domains exit reset, preventing early valid with stale data (cycle 8 actual=2 vs expected=1)."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_f526328dc56f",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 105,
      "mechanism": "input_rst_sync2 is asserted at cycle 2 (output_rst_sync1=1 propagates to input_rst_sync2). Qualifying readiness with input_rst_sync2 (active high) inverts the gate logic: tready=~full && input_rst_sync2 enables ready during reset (cycles 2-4) instead of masking it. This contradicts the required masking behavior and would worsen the cycle-4 tready=1 failure."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The root cause is reset domain crossing: input domain uses 3-cycle synchronizer (input_rst_sync1/2/3) while output domain uses async_rst directly in some checks and output_rst_sync1/2/3 in others. This creates a 4-cycle reset latency mismatch (input exits at cycle 5, output at cycle 1). The top-ranked patches address this by either (1) masking input_axis_tready until output domain acknowledges reset (patch_213cfcafcc24), (2) gating input ready with input reset synchronizer stages (patch_ac60e3091721, patch_e4d895e08cad), (3) coupling output reset exit to input reset completion (patch_3d75e9177240), or (4) gating the data write path with input reset (patch_9425e1a524ed). Patch_f526328dc56f is ranked last as its active-high gate inverts the required masking polarity."
}
